course: dictionary
lesson: 1530
title: Ceremony and formal occasions
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A ceremony is a formal event held to mark something important, such as a wedding, a graduation or a prize-giving. «The school held an award ceremony on the last day of term.» The word tells you the day was special, and people were dressed up and behaving properly.
explain: Ceremony can also mean the set of formal actions and words done in the proper way. «A christening is a church ceremony where a baby is welcomed into the church and given its names.» Look for the clues: church, baby, names. They tell you what happens during the ceremony.
explain: Be careful: «without ceremony» does not mean with a party. It means simply and quickly, with no fuss. Ask yourself what word would fit, then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A ceremony is a formal event held to mark something important.
fact: Examples of ceremonies include weddings, graduations, prize-givings and christenings.
fact: Ceremony can also mean the formal actions and words done in the proper way on such an occasion.
fact: «Without ceremony» means simply and quickly, with no fuss.
fact: «Rite» means a ceremony. It sounds the same as «right» and «write», so check the meaning to choose the correct spelling.
exercise: «The school held an award ceremony on the last day of term.» What is a ceremony? | A type of school lunch | A test with no marks | *A formal event held to mark something important
exercise: «He was welcomed in without ceremony, so he just walked straight in.» What does without ceremony mean here? | With a long, grand speech and music | With a party and lots of games | *Simply and quickly, with no fuss | Very slowly and carefully
exercise: «A christening is a church ceremony where a baby is given its names.» Find and copy one word that means a formal event. | church | *ceremony | christening | baby
test: Which event is a ceremony? | *A graduation where students receive their certificates | A football game in the playground | A walk to the shops | A quick snack at break time
test: «Ireland's President is head of state, mostly for ceremonies.» What does ceremonies mean here? | Lessons in maths | Ceremonies of rain and wind | Silly games for children | *Formal events with set steps and words
exam: Which sentence uses ceremony correctly? | Ceremony is the colour of the paint on the wall | The ceremony ate my sandwich at lunch | *The wedding ceremony was held in the church garden | I won the ceremony by running faster in the playground
